4.2. Synthetic Sample Generation
We generate the sample using the following mainstream models at the time of writing:
GPT-4o, GPT-4o mini, Llama 3.2 (3B), Llama 3.3 (70B), and Gemini 2.5 Flash.
We
avoid overly advanced models, which may perform costly and time-consuming logical
deduction rather than quickly reflecting human choices. Thus, our evidence speaks to
realistic and affordable research practices rather than to a capability ceiling that few
applied researchers can afford to reach, especially as conjoint design tends to involve
longer input. In addition, as an open-source model, Llama allows better reproducibility.
5Note that information about the order/combination of profiles included in each task of each respondent
in Hankinson (2018) is missing.
10


We use a moderate temperature 𝑇= 0.5 as a baseline specification to balance diversity
and predictability. We adopt a one-to-one persona mirroring strategy and benchmark
synthetic agents to reflect the same set of demographic information and profile en-
counters as the human sample. This helps minimize the design variance. Additionally,
we perform an exploratory exercise by tweaking 𝑇∈{0.0, 0.5, 1.0} when testing Hain-
mueller and Hopkins (2015), Hankinson (2018), Ono and Burden (2019), and Teele et al.
(2018), which are chosen based on the sum of profiles included.
4.3. Statistical Tests
4.3.1. Distribution
To evaluate Hypothesis 1, we compare the general choice distributions at joint, marginal,
and individual levels.6 We start with the Wasserstein distance to compare the full joint
distribution of chosen profiles (Kantorovich, 1960):
